# Assist, conversation and voice

Make it clear when Assist is listening, which request it understood and whether a command has a confirmed result. Voice must never be the only way to do a household task, and a theme change must never turn on a microphone, send text or operate a device.

This page completes HA-GAP-025. Its examples are simulated. They do not install a native feature or adapter, and they do not connect to Home Assistant.

## What Home Assistant owns

Assist handles voice and typed requests, using local or remote processing depending on how it is set up. Its pipeline and the entities exposed to it decide what is possible. civica does not replace recognition, conversation agents, authentication or device permissions. See the Home Assistant references at the end of this page.

## What to show

| Source condition | Show | Do not |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Ready or listening | The listening state and the real stop control. | Start recording because of navigation or a theme update. A native voice shortcut the household chose on purpose may start listening. |
| Processing | The submitted request and the stage, where Home Assistant gives one. | Treat recognition as a successful action. |
| Ambiguous target | A request to choose, with the named options. | Pick a device because its name looks similar. |
| Request accepted | What Home Assistant accepted. | Say a lock or light changed before its own confirmation arrives. |
| Not understood, rejected or unconfirmed | Say which of the three it is. | Repeat a consequential request automatically. |

## How the parts fit

1. Offer the native typed route as well as voice. Say where processing happens only from the real configuration. Do not promise that every Assist setup stays local.
2. Expose only what the selected agent, the entities and the account allow. A theme cannot bypass exposure or permission settings.
3. Keep the transcript, the interpretation, the acknowledgement and the reported device state apart. A spoken reply is not enough to show that a device changed.
4. Leave microphone permission, listening and audio playback under native, explicit control. Keep a shortcut that was set up on purpose: native dashboard Assist actions can use `start_listening`. Label it so the person knows it listens. Keep supported interruption. Do not invent a way to cancel a request that was already sent.

## Accessible interaction

- Show listening, processing and error states as text as well as animation or sound, and keep a usable typed route.
- Do not announce each partial recognition result. Announce a completion or an error, and let people read the transcript without playing audio.
- Respect reduced motion and shared-screen privacy. Do not publish real recordings, transcripts or household names as test evidence.

## When something fails

- If microphone access is denied, say which route is affected and offer typing. Do not suggest a theme can grant permission.
- If the target is unclear, keep the request and ask for a specific choice. Do not guess.
- If the connection drops after the request was sent, show the result as unconfirmed and check the device's reported state before trying again.

